{
  "case_id": "2022-22-174",
  "document_hash": "724b7b0fea5d8684997b52d6af04b43e0f717097b88941eb067122dbc289fe8a",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2022-22-174",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "724b7b0fea5d8684997b52d6af04b43e0f717097b88941eb067122dbc289fe8a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-724b7b0fea5d-0000-0000",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 2,
        "start": 2
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "APPEARANCES:\nAARON STREETT, ESQUIRE, Houston, Texas; on behalf of the Petitioner.\nGEN. ELIZABETH B. PRELOGAR, Solicitor General, Department of Justice, Washington, D.C.; on behalf of the Respondent."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Aaron Streett",
      "case_id": "2022-22-174",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "724b7b0fea5d8684997b52d6af04b43e0f717097b88941eb067122dbc289fe8a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-724b7b0fea5d-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Title VII requires religious accommodations absent an undue hardship on the conduct of the employer's business. TWA versus Hardison violates the statute's promise that employees should not be forced to choose between their faith and their job."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Thomas",
      "case_id": "2022-22-174",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "724b7b0fea5d8684997b52d6af04b43e0f717097b88941eb067122dbc289fe8a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-724b7b0fea5d-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 6,
        "start": 6
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE THOMAS: Just a couple of cleanup questions. What was actually decided was the law being considered in Hardison? Was it the -- the Title VII as amended, or was it a guideline?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2022-22-174",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "724b7b0fea5d8684997b52d6af04b43e0f717097b88941eb067122dbc289fe8a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-724b7b0fea5d-0000-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 11,
        "start": 11
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOTOMAYOR: Excuse me. You are really asking us to overrule not just the de minimis test but the entire holdings of Hardison. You appear to be saying that the three holdings of Hardison, as I understood them to be, one, that a employ -- it would be an undue hardship if an employer has to breach its collective bargaining agreement."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2022-22-174",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "724b7b0fea5d8684997b52d6af04b43e0f717097b88941eb067122dbc289fe8a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-724b7b0fea5d-0001-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 31,
        "start": 31
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Do you think that there's anything illegitimate about discounting an argument of -- about congressional acquiescence or congressional inaction when there's good reason to believe that a reasonable member of Congress would think that there would be constitutional problems with adopting the kind of remedial legislation that is posited?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Barrett",
      "case_id": "2022-22-174",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "724b7b0fea5d8684997b52d6af04b43e0f717097b88941eb067122dbc289fe8a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-724b7b0fea5d-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 45,
        "start": 45
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BARRETT: And when do they say\nit's enough?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Barrett",
      "case_id": "2022-22-174",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "724b7b0fea5d8684997b52d6af04b43e0f717097b88941eb067122dbc289fe8a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-724b7b0fea5d-0002-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 45,
        "start": 45
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BARRETT: But it sounds to me\nlike you're saying morale is not enough unless\nsomeone actually quits. So, you know, if on\nFriday it's very clear to the employer that\nmorale is at an all-time low, it -- it's not --\nit's not good enough, but on Monday, after one\nemployee is actually driven to quit, then it's\nenough?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "General Prelogar",
      "case_id": "2022-22-174",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "724b7b0fea5d8684997b52d6af04b43e0f717097b88941eb067122dbc289fe8a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-724b7b0fea5d-0003-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 68,
        "start": 68
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I think, if this Court made clear that the \"de minimis\" language should not be taken literally to mean every dollar above a trifle is immunizing the employers from liability, that is absolutely a correct statement of the law. It's consistent with Hardison."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2022-22-174",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "724b7b0fea5d8684997b52d6af04b43e0f717097b88941eb067122dbc289fe8a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-724b7b0fea5d-0003-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 69,
        "start": 69
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KAGAN: So there would be no basis for vacating and remand in light of this universal agreement that we're not talking about trifles?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Barrett",
      "case_id": "2022-22-174",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "724b7b0fea5d8684997b52d6af04b43e0f717097b88941eb067122dbc289fe8a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-724b7b0fea5d-0003-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 70,
        "start": 70
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BARRETT: But why wouldn't we vacate and remand to let the Third Circuit know -- like let's imagine that we took Justice Gorsuch's approach and said, you know, to be clear -- and I think lots of courts of appeals are and, in fact, the EEOC guidelines for employers, the more informal sheet, says anything but minimal costs. That makes it sound like trifling."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kavanaugh",
      "case_id": "2022-22-174",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "724b7b0fea5d8684997b52d6af04b43e0f717097b88941eb067122dbc289fe8a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-724b7b0fea5d-0003-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 86,
        "start": 86
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KAVANAUGH: And, of course, applying that to a particular set of facts is challenging, as Justice Alito's questions and others have pointed out, but that's the line you would draw in the first bucket?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2022-22-174",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "724b7b0fea5d8684997b52d6af04b43e0f717097b88941eb067122dbc289fe8a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-724b7b0fea5d-0004-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 92,
        "start": 92
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "What in your view is the relevance of the fact that a requested accommodation would be inconsistent with a provision of a collective bargaining agreement or a memorandum of understanding that doesn't have anything to do with seniority?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Gorsuch",
      "case_id": "2022-22-174",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "724b7b0fea5d8684997b52d6af04b43e0f717097b88941eb067122dbc289fe8a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-724b7b0fea5d-0004-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 106,
        "start": 106
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "But what if -- what if the facts are that an employer has to pay a premium wage to get anybody to work on Saturday or Sunday, and you do have a religious employee who wants to take either Saturday or Sunday off because of their sincerely held religious beliefs so that, yes, the employer is always going to have to pay a premium wage, but it's going to have to pay a premium wage for Saturday and Sunday work no matter what, because it's just hard to get anybody to work those days because some people want to go to church and others want to go to their kids' soccer games. Would that be proof enough for the employer to escape undue burden under your -- under your test?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kavanaugh",
      "case_id": "2022-22-174",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "724b7b0fea5d8684997b52d6af04b43e0f717097b88941eb067122dbc289fe8a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-724b7b0fea5d-0004-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 107,
        "start": 107
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "First of all, on substantial costs, that was in Footnote 14, that's, I think, responding to the dissent's concern in Hardison and saying substantial costs. Do you agree that that's the same as more than de minimis costs for purposes of Hardison?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "General Prelogar",
      "case_id": "2022-22-174",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "724b7b0fea5d8684997b52d6af04b43e0f717097b88941eb067122dbc289fe8a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-724b7b0fea5d-0004-0001",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 107,
        "start": 107
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Yes, I think the Court was using those terms interchangeably."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "General Prelogar",
      "case_id": "2022-22-174",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "724b7b0fea5d8684997b52d6af04b43e0f717097b88941eb067122dbc289fe8a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-724b7b0fea5d-0005-0003",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 108,
        "start": 108
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The EEOC and the lower courts from\n1980 onwards for more than 40 years have\nproperly applied Hardison in light of its facts."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kavanaugh",
      "case_id": "2022-22-174",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "724b7b0fea5d8684997b52d6af04b43e0f717097b88941eb067122dbc289fe8a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-724b7b0fea5d-0005-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 109,
        "start": 109
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KAVANAUGH: Do you understand\n\"undue hardship\" -- I understand that term in\nthe original statute to reflect a balance\nbetween two important values: one, religious\nliberty and the other the rights of American\nbusinesses to thrive, and to thrive, you have to\nbe able to make money.\nIs that how you understand \"undue\nhardship\"?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "General Prelogar",
      "case_id": "2022-22-174",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "724b7b0fea5d8684997b52d6af04b43e0f717097b88941eb067122dbc289fe8a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-724b7b0fea5d-0005-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 110,
        "start": 110
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "GENERAL PRELOGAR: We think that it\nabsolutely controls this case. The district\ncourt squarely held and there is no way to get\naround the district court's factual findings\nabout the -- the -- or its understanding of the\nmeaning of the MOU in this case, because I think\nthat it's evident from its plain terms that the\nMOU created the strict rotation system for\nAmazon's Sunday delivery."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Aaron Streett",
      "case_id": "2022-22-174",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "724b7b0fea5d8684997b52d6af04b43e0f717097b88941eb067122dbc289fe8a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-724b7b0fea5d-0005-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 120,
        "start": 120
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. STREETT: This Court should not\napply the strong medicine of statutory\nstare decisis where it's, at best, unclear that\nthe Court had before it in Hardison the current\nversion of the statute, and it certainly should\nnot apply those doctrines when the government is\nnot even defending the test by its terms or\ndefending the neutrality rationale of Hardison."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2022-22-174",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "724b7b0fea5d8684997b52d6af04b43e0f717097b88941eb067122dbc289fe8a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-724b7b0fea5d-0005-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 123,
        "start": 123
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: Thank you,\ncounsel. The case is submitted."
    },
    {
